            <ArticleTitle>Restless Leg Syndrome/Willis-Ekbom Disease Pathophysiology.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>207-14, xi</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jsmc.2015.05.022</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1556-407X(15)00057-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Restless leg syndrome/Willis-Ekbom disease has brain iron deficiency that produces excessive dopamine and known genetic risks, some of which contribute to the brain iron deficiency. Dopamine treatments work temporarily but may eventually produce further postsynaptic down-regulation and worse restless leg syndrome. This article includes sections focused on pathophysiologic findings from each of these areas: genetics, cortical-spinal excitability, and iron and dopamine.</AbstractText>
